p.src:
With $x$ measured from equilibrium, momentum $p$, mass $m$, and spring constant $\kappa$, the Hamiltonian is $H(x,p)=\frac{p^2}{2m}+\frac{\kappa x^2}{2},
 \qquad
 \omega=\sqrt{\frac{\kappa}{m}}.$ The bath fixes $T$, and from this point we again use $k_B=1$, so $\beta=1/T$. An oscillator equilibrated with the room has a temperature near $300\ {\rm K}$; one immersed in liquid helium has a temperature of only a few kelvins. A putative spring placed in the center of the Sun would not survive as a spring, but the example makes the role of the environment clear. The Boltzmann weight factorizes: $e^{-\beta H}
 =e^{-\beta p^2/(2m)}
  e^{-\beta\kappa x^2/2}.$

