p.src:
Response: The physical levels are $E_n=\left(n+\frac12\right)\hbar\omega.$ Consequently $\begin{aligned}
 Z_{\rm phys}
 &=e^{-\beta\hbar\omega/2}Z_{\rm exc},\\
 E_{\rm phys}
 &=\frac{\hbar\omega}{2}
  +\frac{\hbar\omega}{e^{\beta\hbar\omega}-1}.
 \end{aligned}$ The common shift does not change level probabilities, entropy, heat capacity, or thermal excitation energy, but it does shift the absolute mean energy by $\hbar\omega/2$. The multiplying factor in $Z$ is $\beta$-dependent, so it must not be discarded when absolute energy is wanted.  (Lecture timestamp: 01:11:08–01:11:37)

